  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    A compact young Nightshelf courier active during the founding era, known by their trade-name per Courier Corps convention. Splitpath's flipper-ink glowed a steady amber in both light and dark — the luminescent seal pressed into certified manifests. Ink-marks bearing their signature persist on founding-era parchment from early fire-core shipments bound for surface colonies, which is to say the seal outlasted the courier.
    
    Splitpath ran regular eight-day routes through Midway Shelf and between the Berg's deep tunnels and surface markets, carrying trade correspondence and fire-core shipment confirmations. They were known for strict discretion regarding private exchanges carried alongside official cargo — a reputation that attracted increasingly sensitive commissions.
    
    Among these were sealed letters between Depth-Purveyor ☽'Cilocum, a Nightshelf official, and ☽'Otrolal the Watchful at the deep warren of ☽'slew Nisaemesius~ — correspondence carried past the seventeen skeletons that mark the deepest stretch of the Midway Shelf route. In the seasons before Shadow-Broker Nyla's confession to the Aurora Stack council, Splitpath reported weight discrepancies in sealed fire-core crates to council member Amaang Skael'Compass — crates running heavier than their manifests across two consecutive seasons. That Splitpath brought this information to Amaang specifically is worth noting, given what subsequent events revealed about Amaang's own activities.
    
    During the Aurora Stack succession crisis, Splitpath carried a sealed crate from the Nightshelf warren of ☽'scar Ramtatus~ to the Aerie Perch, an Aurora Stack overlook, logged as aurora-crystal samples but containing tunnel-condition reports and deep-warren structural intelligence. This is the act that places Splitpath outside legitimate courier function.
    
    During a supply interruption at the colony of Promenade Hei, Splitpath delivered eleven fire-cores instead of the requisite fourteen, citing the Nightshelf's reading of the Long-dark Edict's supply clause against a pre-Darkening baseline. The colony expected the post-Darkening adjusted figure. Splitpath delivered what the Nightshelf authorized.
    
    Splitpath died before the orca incursions reached ☽'slew Ravium~. Their name was scratched into junction ice under the Vestigium∴moria memorial practice, spoken by a Midnight Claw operative who had known them.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771593003929_0", anchor: "what subsequent events revealed about Amaang's own activities", text: "Splitpath brought evidence of cargo tampering to the one council member that evidence would eventually implicate. The entry flags this but does not follow the thread. Neither did I. Three consecutive seasons of fire-core transfer logs are missing from administrative ice at this stratum — not degraded, not illegible, but absent in the specific way that indicates deliberate removal. Someone edited the record. The ice remembers the shape of what was taken. It does not remember the content. Splitpath's report went somewhere. Not where they intended.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771593003929_1", anchor: "eleven fire-cores instead of the requisite fourteen", text: "Three cores short. At standard burn-rate for a colony Promenade Hei's size, that is nine to twelve days of heating deficit distributed across the settlement. Splitpath's legal reading was correct — the pre-Darkening baseline does support eleven. The post-Darkening adjustment Promenade Hei expected was never formally ratified; it circulated as convention, not statute. Splitpath delivered what the law required. The law required people to be cold.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771593003929_2", anchor: "spoken by a Midnight Claw operative who had known them", text: "The Vestigium∴moria asks only this: that someone who knew the dead speak their name at a tunnel junction, so the ice carries it forward. Not a superior. Not a guild-master. A Claw enforcer — from an organization that would later contest control of the tunnels Splitpath walked every eight days. The practice does not ask for rank or eulogy. It asks for recognition. Someone recognized Splitpath. Scratched the name. Kept walking. That is the entire record of their mourning, and it is enough, and it will outlast this entry.", type: :commentary, display: :full
